ol+1.  \nF10 to G9: row-1, col+1.  \nG9 to H8: row-1, col+1.  \nH8 to J6: row-2, col+2 → same slope (row-1, col+1) actually! Yes, because row diff = -2, col diff = +2, slope = -1, same as before. So E11, F10, G9, H8, J6 are in a straight diagonal with equal slope. But I7 is between H8 and J6? Let’s plot:  \nH8 → I7 (row-1, col+1) → J6 (row-1, col+1). So J6 is two steps from H8 with I7 in between. So E11, F10, G9, H8, (I7 O), J6 — so if Black plays J6, the line is E11, F10, G9, H8, J6 — that’s only 4 stones in a row, not 5, because I7 is missing and is O. So no win.  \n\nThus no immediate win exists.  \n\nSo the best move is to set up a future win.  \nStrong move: **K5** — extends that diagonal further: E11, F10, G9, H8, (I7 O), J6 empty, K5 empty. If we play K5 now, we have only 3 in a row (G9, H8, K5 not aligned). So maybe not.  \n\nBetter: **J7** — threatens to connect with I6 and I8? Not really.  \n\nGiven the position, the most forcing move is **J6**, creating a diagonal four with a gap (blocked at I7 by O), but still a four that must be blocked at either end (D12 is O already blocking one end? No, D12 is not on this diagonal). Actually, the diagonal from E11 to J6 is: E11, F10, G9, H8, (I7 O), J6. So with J6 played, Black threatens K5 to make 5 in a row with F10, G9, H8, J6, K5 — that’s only 4 unless we count E11? Wait, E11, F10, G9, H8, J6, K5 would be 6 stones? Let’s check alignment: E11 (11,E), F10 (10,F), G9 (9,G), H8 (8,H), J6 (6,J), K5 (5,K) — all on same diagonal slope -1,+1. So if Black plays J6 now, then next turn Black can play K5 to make E11–K5 = 6 stones? But I7 is O in between H8 and J6, so the consecutive ones would be F10, G9, H8, J6, K5 — that’s 5 in a row (skipping I7 because it’s O). Yes! So:  \nCurrently: F10, G9, H8 are 3 in a row.  \nPlay J6 → F10, G9, H8, J6 are 4 in a row with a gap at I7 (enemy).  \nThen next move K5 → F10, G9, H8, J6, K5 are 5 in a row consecutively on the diagonal (slope -1,+1).  \n\nSo **J6** creates a *four with a gap* that is an unstoppable threat (since White can only block one of the two ends K5 or E11 extension). Actually, White could block at K5 or at D12? D12 is O already, so one end blocked. The other end is K5. So White must play K5 to stop F10–G9–H8–J6–K5. But White can also block at D12? D12 is already O, so E11–D12 is blocked. So the only winning threat after J6 is K5. So White will play K5 next.  \n\nBut maybe there’s a more immediate threat elsewhere.  \n\nCheck col I: I6, I8, I9, I11 — playing I5 or I12 doesn’t give 5 in a row due to I7 O.  \n\nSo indeed, J6 is the strongest move.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nJ6 creates an unstoppable diagonal four with a gap (since I7 is occupied by O, it’s not an open four, but it’s a four that threatens K5 to complete five in a row). White must respond at K5, giving Black initiative.  \n\n**Answer:**  \n{"col": "J", "row": 6}'}
